  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14253467/assets/b165889c6169a417f4d56f544aec8a049685693ff2b01ea13d51a9f4d841be4c.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve (shown above)\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-4,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\n find the derivative $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ at the point $P$ on the curve where $t=3.$",
  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-1,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2t}{3{t}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=3$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3}{3({3}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{27-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
